from biology_qa_writer import q,write_bank
S='4.3.'
items=[
q(1,'What is a pathogen?','A microorganism that causes disease.',S+'1.1'),
q(1,'Which type of pathogen causes malaria?','A protist.',S+'1.5'),
q(1,'Name the cell formed by fusing an antibody-producing lymphocyte with a tumour cell.','Hybridoma.',S+'2.1','2023 Q04.4'),
q(1,'Name the scientist who discovered penicillin.','Alexander Fleming.',S+'1.9'),
q(1,'Which plant was the original source of chemicals used to develop aspirin?','Willow.',S+'1.9','2023 Q01.3'),
q(1,'Name the yellowing of leaves caused by magnesium deficiency.','Chlorosis.',S+'3.1','2024 Q07.4–07.5'),
q(1,'What does a valid control line on an antibody test strip show?','That the test has worked.',S+'2.2','2023 Q04.7'),
q(2,'Give two ways bacteria can cause illness after entering the body.','- They **multiply rapidly**, increasing the number of bacteria present.\n- They may release **toxins** that damage tissues.',S+'1.1','2023 Q03.1'),
q(2,'Describe how measles spreads and give one characteristic symptom.','- It spreads when people **inhale droplets** from infected people’s coughs or sneezes.\n- A **red skin rash** is a characteristic symptom; fever also occurs.',S+'1.2'),
q(2,'Give two ways to reduce the transmission of gonorrhoea.','- Use **condoms** to reduce transmission during sexual contact.\n- Treat infected people with an **effective antibiotic**, reducing the bacteria available to spread.',S+'1.3'),
q(2,'Explain why mosquito nets help reduce malaria transmission.','- Mosquitoes are **vectors** carrying the malaria protist.\n- Nets reduce mosquito bites, so fewer protists are transferred into people.',S+'1.5'),
q(2,'Explain how cilia and mucus protect the airways.','- **Mucus traps** pathogens and particles.\n- **Cilia move the mucus towards the throat** to be swallowed.',S+'1.6'),
q(2,'Explain the difference between antibodies and antitoxins.','- **Antibodies bind to specific antigens** on pathogens, helping the immune system destroy them.\n- **Antitoxins neutralise toxins** released by bacteria.',S+'1.6'),
q(2,'Why do antibiotics not treat influenza, and why is developing drugs against viruses difficult?','- Influenza is caused by a **virus**, which antibiotics do not kill.\n- Viruses reproduce **inside body cells**, so a drug may damage the body’s cells as well as affecting the virus.',S+'1.8','2024 Q06.4–06.5'),
q(2,'Explain why antibiotic resistance is a concern.','- Some bacteria **survive existing antibiotics**, making infections harder to treat.\n- Developing effective replacements takes **time and money**, while infections may continue to spread.',S+'1.8','2024 Q06.3'),
q(2,'Explain why a nitrate shortage can cause stunted plant growth.','- Nitrate ions are needed to make **amino acids and proteins**.\n- Without enough protein synthesis, fewer new cell materials can be made, so **growth is reduced**.',S+'3.1','2022 Q04.3'),
q(2,'Explain how a waxy cuticle and poisonous chemicals protect a plant.','- The tough cuticle is a **physical barrier** resisting entry by microorganisms.\n- Poisons **deter herbivores** from feeding on the plant.',S+'3.2','2022 Q04.1'),
q(3,'Describe the progression from HIV infection to AIDS if infection is not successfully controlled.','- HIV may initially cause a **flu-like illness**.\n- The virus attacks and damages **immune cells**.\n- Severe immune damage leads to **AIDS**, when other infections or cancers can develop because defence is weakened.',S+'1.2','2022 Q07.1–07.3; 2024 Q06.6'),
q(3,'Explain how removing infected leaves and applying fungicide help control rose black spot.','- Rose black spot is caused by a **fungus**.\n- Removing and destroying infected leaves removes a source of fungal spores, reducing spread by **wind or water**.\n- A **fungicide** acts against the fungus, reducing infection.',S+'1.4','2022 Q02.2'),
q(3,'Explain why magnesium deficiency reduces a plant’s growth.','- Magnesium is needed to make **chlorophyll**.\n- Less chlorophyll means less light absorbed and a lower rate of **photosynthesis**.\n- Less glucose is available for respiration and making new plant material, so growth slows.',S+'3.1','2022 Q04.3; 2024 Q07.4–07.5'),
q(3,'Explain why a monoclonal antibody is useful for locating one particular protein in a tissue sample.','- Its binding site is **specific/complementary** to an antigen on that protein.\n- Attach a **fluorescent dye** to the antibody so it binds to the target protein.\n- The fluorescent signal shows **where the protein is located**.',S+'2.2','2022 Q07.8; 2023 Q04.6'),
q(3,'Explain how a hybridoma is used to produce a large supply of one antibody.','- Select a hybridoma making the required antibody and **clone it**.\n- Many identical cells divide and produce **the same antibody**.\n- Collect the antibodies from the culture and **purify** them.',S+'2.1','2023 Q04.5'),
q(3,'A town records 54 cases of an infection among 18,000 people. Calculate cases per 100,000 people and explain why this measure helps compare towns.','- 54 ÷ 18,000 × 100,000.\n- **300 cases per 100,000 people**.\n- It adjusts for different **population sizes**, making the comparison fairer than raw case counts.',S+'1.1','2022 Q07.5'),
q(3,'A plant has yellow leaves. Give three ways of investigating whether a specific pathogen is responsible.','- Compare its symptoms with a reliable **gardening manual or website**.\n- Send a sample to a **laboratory** for pathogen identification.\n- Use a **monoclonal-antibody test kit** for that pathogen; combine the result with other evidence because yellowing can also result from mineral deficiency.',S+'3.1'),
q(4,'Explain how vaccination can prevent illness when the same pathogen enters the body later.','- The vaccine introduces **dead/inactive pathogen or its antigens**.\n- White blood cells make **specific antibodies** against those antigens.\n- **Memory cells** remain and produce a faster, larger antibody response on later exposure.\n- The pathogen is destroyed before it can cause significant illness.',S+'1.7','2022 Q07.6; 2023 Q03.2'),
q(4,'Explain how vaccination of poultry against Salmonella can reduce food poisoning in humans.','- Vaccination stimulates poultry to produce **specific antibodies** to Salmonella antigens.\n- Later infection triggers a **rapid antibody response** that helps destroy bacteria.\n- Fewer bacteria contaminate poultry products such as meat or eggs.\n- People swallow fewer bacteria, reducing infection and the toxins produced in their bodies.',S+'1.3–4.3.1.7','2023 Q03.2'),
q(4,'A pregnancy test uses coloured antibodies that bind a hormone in urine. The hormone–antibody complexes are trapped at a test line. Another line acts as a control. Explain a positive result and why a strip with no control line is invalid.','- The hormone binds to antibodies with a **complementary binding site**.\n- The complexes are trapped at the test line, where the **coloured antibodies build up**, giving a visible line.\n- A control line shows the test has functioned, for example that liquid and antibodies have moved along the strip.\n- Without a control line, a missing test line could be caused by test failure, so it cannot be interpreted as a reliable negative result.',S+'2.2','2023 Q04.6–04.9'),
q(4,'In a trial, 84 of 120 patients improve with a drug and 60 of 120 improve with a placebo. Calculate both improvement rates and the difference in percentage points. Give one reason these data alone do not show whether the drug should be used.','- Drug: 84 ÷ 120 × 100 = **70%**.\n- Placebo: 60 ÷ 120 × 100 = **50%**.\n- Difference = **20 percentage points**.\n- We also need evidence about **side effects**, or whether the sample and allocation provide a fair, reliable comparison.',S+'1.9'),
q(4,'Explain how TMV and aphids can each reduce plant growth.','- TMV causes **mosaic leaf discolouration**, reducing photosynthesis.\n- Less glucose is available for respiration and building new cell material.\n- Aphids feed on the plant’s **sugar-rich sap**.\n- This removes resources needed for growth and respiration.',S+'1.2–4.3.3.1'),
q(6,'Describe how a new drug is tested before it is used widely. Explain why controls are needed in clinical trials.','- Carry out **preclinical tests** on cells, tissues and live animals to investigate effects and potential harm.\n- Begin clinical trials with **very low doses in healthy volunteers**, monitoring safety and side effects.\n- Test on **patients** to assess effectiveness and find the **optimum dose**. Check toxicity, efficacy and dose throughout.\n- Randomly allocate people to comparable groups; use a **placebo or appropriate comparison treatment** to separate drug effects from expectations and natural recovery.\n- Make the trial **double-blind** so patients and assessors do not know allocations, reducing bias. Use a large enough sample and compare side effects as well as benefits.\n- Submit methods and results for **peer review**, so other scientists can scrutinise the evidence before publication.',S+'1.9','2023 Q01.7–01.9; 2024 Q08.6'),
q(6,'Describe how monoclonal antibodies are produced and explain how they can deliver a toxic drug to cancer cells.','- Introduce the target antigen into a mouse to stimulate **antibody-producing lymphocytes**.\n- Collect lymphocytes and fuse them with tumour cells to form **hybridomas**, combining antibody production with repeated division.\n- Select a hybridoma making the required antibody and **clone** it.\n- Grow the clone and **collect and purify** its identical antibodies.\n- Attach a toxic drug to an antibody whose binding site is **complementary to an antigen on the cancer cells**.\n- The antibody binds to those cells and delivers the drug to them, reducing damage to other cells compared with a less selective delivery method; side effects can still occur.',S+'2.1–4.3.2.2','2022 Q07.7–07.8; 2023 Q04.4–04.6'),
q(6,'A vaccination programme reduces infection rates, but uptake is low in some areas. Explain how vaccination protects individuals and why improving uptake may protect other people too.','- Vaccine antigens stimulate white blood cells to make **specific antibodies**.\n- **Memory cells** remain, allowing a faster and larger response to the same pathogen later.\n- Rapid destruction of the pathogen reduces the chance of significant illness.\n- When many people are immune, an infected person meets fewer **susceptible hosts**, so transmission chains are interrupted.\n- This helps protect people who cannot be vaccinated, although protection is not guaranteed.\n- Low-uptake areas contain more susceptible hosts, so outbreaks are more likely; improving access and giving reliable information about benefits and risks can increase protection.',S+'1.7','2022 Q07.6; 2023 Q03.2'),
q(6,'Compare physical, chemical and mechanical plant defences. Give examples and explain how each helps.','- **Cellulose cell walls** form a physical barrier to invading microorganisms.\n- A **tough waxy cuticle** protects leaf surfaces; dead bark protects stems and shedding it can remove pathogens.\n- **Antibacterial chemicals** act against bacteria.\n- **Poisons** discourage herbivores from eating the plant.\n- **Thorns and hairs** make feeding more difficult or unpleasant; leaves that droop or curl when touched can also deter animals.\n- **Mimicry** can trick animals into avoiding the plant. These adaptations act in different ways, rather than providing an antibody-based immune response.',S+'3.2','2022 Q04.1–04.2')]
write_bank('03 - Infection and response','Infection and response',items,'- Vaccination: 2022 Q07.6; 2023 Q03.2.\n- Monoclonal antibody production and uses: 2022 Q07.7–07.8; 2023 Q04.4–04.9.\n- Drug testing: 2023 Q01.7–01.9; 2024 Q08.6.\n- Plant mineral deficiencies: 2022 Q04.3; 2024 Q07.4–07.5.\n- Further examples: plant defences (2022 Q04.1–04.2) and antibiotic resistance/viral treatment (2024 Q06.1–06.6).\n\nThe first four themes occur in at least two of the three sampled papers. Other questions fill specification coverage; all wording and mark allocations in this bank are original.')
